COPY public.submission_auditor_assignments (id, submission_id, auditor_id, auditor_name, auditor_email, auditor_type, category, assigned_at, post, status, submitted_at, values_data, tables_data, attachments, review_status, requires_auditor_resubmission, correction_requested_for_auditor, auditor_correction_requested, auditor_correction_message, auditor_correction_requested_by, auditor_correction_requested_on) FROM stdin;
48	77	262	Dr Arun Sharma	arunsharma@igdtuw.ac.in	external	academic	2026-07-28 11:28:12.22209	SOCE	SUBMITTED	2026-07-30 19:23:20.743079	{"schoolName":"School of Continuing Education","establishmentYear":"2023","address":"D Y Patil International University, Sector 29, Akurdi, Pune,411044","directorName":"Dr Swapnil Bhurat","directorEmail":"swapnil.bhurat@dypiu.ac.in","ugIntake":"150","pgIntake":"60","academicCalendar":"Available","bosMeetingsCount":"2","stakeholderFeedback":"Based on the employer feedback forms from Gill Plants Pvt. Ltd., Dana Anand India Pvt. Ltd., and Mahle Anand Thermal Systems, the overall feedback is positive. Employers consistently rated the curriculum, graduate preparedness, technical competence, communication, teamwork, and professionalism highly (mostly 4–5 on a 5-point scale). Their suggestions mainly focus on enhancing practical exposure, industry-specific software, certification programmes, and application-oriented learning.","feedbackAnalysis":"The overall feedback received from students, alumni, and employers was highly positive, indicating that the curriculum is relevant, comprehensive, and aligned with current industry requirements.\\nStudents, alumni, and employers appreciated the strong theoretical foundation, industry-oriented curriculum, and outcome-based teaching-learning process, which has contributed to improved employability and professional competence.\\nA significant proportion of respondents expressed satisfaction with the curriculum relevance, assessment methods, practical learning, communication skills, and graduate preparedness for employment and higher studies.\\nStakeholders recommended greater emphasis on practical learning, laboratory sessions, industrial visits, internships, live projects, and industry-sponsored activities to further strengthen experiential learning.\\nFeedback highlighted the need to continuously incorporate emerging technologies such as Artificial Intelligence (AI), Machine Learning (ML), Data Analytics, Industry 4.0, Robotics, Automation, Electric Vehicle technologies, CAD/CAE tools, Power BI, PLC programming, MATLAB/Simulink, and digital engineering tools into the curriculum.\\nEmployers acknowledged that graduates possess good technical knowledge, professional ethics, teamwork, and problem-solving abilities, while recommending more application-oriented learning and industrial exposure.\\nOverall, the feedback indicates that the curriculum effectively meets stakeholder expectations while identifying opportunities for continuous curriculum enhancement through stronger industry collaboration, practical exposure, emerging technology integration, and skill-based learning, thereby further improving graduate employability and programme quality.","actionTakenReport":"The feedback received from students, alumni, and employers has been systematically analyzed and utilized for continuous curriculum improvement. The recommended actions focus on enhancing industry relevance, practical exposure, emerging technologies, employability skills, and stakeholder engagement, thereby supporting the university's commitment to Outcome-Based Education (OBE) and continuous quality improvement.","foreignCollaborations":"1.\\tMoon Mission\\nTeam Members:  Mr. Dinesh Kumar, Dr. Swapnil Bhurat, Dr. Gaurav Kumar Singh\\n\\nPartner\\tRole & Expertise\\tKey Background\\nDr. Lutz Richter (Germany/ESA Expert)\\tPlanetary Robotics & Mobility\\tA veteran of ESA, NASA, and JAXA missions (ExoMars, Rosetta). He is a specialist in terramechanics—how rover wheels interact with lunar/Martian soil.\\nDr. Tze Chuen (T.C.) Ng (Hong Kong)\\tPrecision Sampling Tools\\tKnown for designing \\"space dental drills\\" and sampling tools used in major missions like Beagle 2 and the Chinese Chang’e lunar missions.\\nDr. Prabhat Ranjan (DYPIU, India)\\tSystems Integration & In Situ Research\\tFormer Vice Chancellor of D.Y. Patil International University (DYPIU). He brings a background in nuclear fusion, sensor networks, and \\"Technology Vision\\" for long-term space research.\\nKey Research Areas\\nThe synergy between these three experts focuses on the \\"In Situ Resource Utilization\\" (ISRU) cycle—finding, excavating, and testing materials on the Moon's surface.\\n1. Lunar Excavation & Mineral Extraction\\nThe collaboration works on the mechanics of excavating lunar regolith (soil). Dr. Richter’s expertise in how rovers move on dusty, low-gravity terrain is paired with Dr. T.C. Ng’s specialized tools to create systems that can dig for minerals without getting stuck or damaging the equipment.\\n2. Advanced Space Sampling Tools\\nDr. T.C. Ng’s work is pivotal here. Unlike standard drills, space tools must operate in a vacuum with extreme temperature swings. The research focuses on:\\n•\\tMicro-sampling: Taking tiny, precise cores of lunar rock.\\n•\\tContamination-free retrieval: Ensuring minerals are excavated and stored for testing without being altered by the tool itself.\\n3. In Situ Testing and Analysis\\nRather than just bringing samples back to Earth, the collaboration explores how to test minerals on the Moon. This includes:\\n•\\tIdentifying volatiles (like water ice) in the lunar polar regions.\\n•\\tTesting the structural properties of regolith for potential use in 3D printing lunar bases.\\n4. Space Rover Autonomy\\nUnder Dr Prabhat Ranjan’s guidance at DYPIU, the research incorporates advanced sensor networks and system logic to allow rovers to operate more autonomously. This is crucial for excavating areas where real-time control from Earth is difficult due to signal lag.\\n\\nResearch Paper: (Dr Ram Kunwer)\\nKumar, A., Kunwer, R., Kanojia, N. et al. Thermal and hydrodynamic characteristics of Therminol VP-1 oil flow across perforated conical hollow turbulence promoter in Scheffler dish receiver tube. Sci Rep 15, 45720 (2025). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-28251-8","nationalCollaborations":"","functionalMousCount":"5","auditObservations":"School of Continuing Education - For Working Professional has established a sound academic ecosystem in a short time supported by adequate physical infrastructure and well-developed laboratory facilities, particularly in the areas of Smart Manufacturing and Electric Vehicle (EV) technologies. These facilities have the potential to provide students with meaningful hands-on learning experiences and support practical skill development aligned with contemporary engineering practices. The School has also made commendable efforts to strengthen industry-academia interaction through collaborations with reputed industries, which are reflected in opportunities for internships, expert interactions, industrial exposure, and collaborative academic activities. The introduction of B.Tech. programs in Mechanical and Electrical Engineering  and MTech in Electric Vehicle for working professionals demonstrates an encouraging initiative towards addressing the learning requirements of the employed workforce and promoting flexible higher education.","auditRecommendations":"During the audit, it was observed that the understanding and implementation of Outcome-Based Education (OBE) require considerable strengthening. Although OBE-related practices have been initiated, there is limited evidence of a systematic approach towards defining measurable learning outcomes, mapping Course Outcomes with Program Outcomes, analyzing attainment levels, and utilizing the findings for continuous academic improvement. In addition, the quality and consistency of academic documentation, including course files, assessment records, and supporting evidence for various academic processes, vary across departments, indicating the need for greater standardization and improved documentation practices across the University. ATR on various feedbacks in few cases also lacks clarity.","auditDocumentation":[],"__reportArchive":{"category":"internal","auditCycle":"2025-26","version":1},"__auditSignOff":{"submittedBy":{"name":"Dr Swapnil Bhurat","designation":"Director","role":"director","date":"2026-07-28T11:27:42.228Z"},"approvedBy":{"name":"","designation":"","role":"","date":""},"auditedBy":{"name":"Dr Arun Sharma","designation":"External Academic Auditor","role":"academic-external-auditor","email":"arunsharma@igdtuw.ac.in","date":"2026-07-30T19:23:17.911Z"}}}	{"studentStrength":[{"Sr No":"1","Class":"B. Tech (ME) 2025-28","No. of Student":"42","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"2","Class":"B. Tech (ME) 2024-27","No. of Student":"75","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"3","Class":"B. Tech (ME) 2023-26","No. of Student":"83","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"4","Class":"B. Tech (EE) 2025-28","No. of Student":"40","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"5","Class":"B. Tech (EE) 2024-27","No. of Student":"26","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"6","Class":"B. Tech (EE) 2023-26","No. of Student":"147","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"7","Class":"M. Tech (EV) 2025-27","No. of Student":"40","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"8","Class":"M. Tech (EV) 2024-26","No. of Student":"33","Total":"486"}],"facultyStrength":[{"Sr No":"1","Required":"24","Available":"12"}],"boardOfStudies":[{"Sr No":"1","Date of the meeting":"30/08/2025 and 13/04/2026","Link for MoM":[{"name":"SoCE BoS Summary.docx.pdf","fileName":"SoCE BoS Summary.docx.pdf","url":"https://storage.googleapis.com/dypiu-schoolappraisal-uploads/users/d291669ed87e0b9f/attachments/d4af52bd-c95d-4f6f-a979-250c685a7c19-SoCE_BoS_Summary.docx.pdf"}]}],"syllabusRevision":[{"Sr No":"1","Category of Feedback":"Student feedback\\t","Link for Analysis and ATR":[{"name":"Curriculum Feedback Form for Students.pdf","fileName":"Curriculum Feedback Form for Students.pdf","url":"https://storage.googleapis.com/dypiu-schoolappraisal-uploads/users/d291669ed87e0b9f/attachments/4d0b7bb3-7c6b-46de-acec-635bc4fca46d-Curriculum_Feedback_Form_for_Students.pdf"}]},{"Sr No":"2","Category of Feedback":"Faculty feedback\\t","Link for Analysis and ATR":[{"name":"SoCE Course Feedback Summary.docx.pdf","fileName":"SoCE Course Feedback Summary.docx.pdf