odies selected by phage display technology exhibit distinct Zika virus binding profiles and minimal cross-reactivity with dengue virus serotype 2\\\\t\\\\nHernan H.M. da Costa, Isaura B.B. Silva, Renato K.A.O. Franca, Carlos H.R. Gomes, Luciana M. Sergio, Marcelo M. Brigido, Andrea Q. Maranhao, Ricardo J.S. Torquato, Elizabeth de Gaspari, Denise C.A. Oliveira, Viviane F. Botosso, Renato M. Astray, Carlos R. Prudencio\\\\t\\\\nInstituto Adolfo Lutz; Butantan Institute; University of Sao Paulo; University of Brasilia, Brazil\\\\n","nationalCollaborations":"22\\\\n1. Dr. Prafull Chavan\\\\t\\\\nCoconut haustorium characterization, preservation, and Products (Book Chapter)\\\\nPooja Sawant\\\\t\\\\nHospital & Post Graduate Institute, P. D. Jain Homeopathic Medical College, Parbhani, Maharashtra, India\\\\n\\\\n2. Dr. Sonal Mahajan\\\\t\\\\nDrug resistance mechanism in intracellular pathogens and modulation (Book Chapter)\\\\t\\\\nAparna K. Dhavade1, Sarika Hinge2, Roopa Nair3\\\\t\\\\n1 Department of Biotechnology, Pune, Maharashtra, India\\\\n2 Department of Physics, Savitribai Phule Pune University, Pune, Maharashtra, India\\\\n3 Springer Nature, Pune, Maharashtra, India\\\\n\\\\n3. Dr. Prafull Chavan\\\\t\\\\nFinger Millet Food Product Development (Book Chapter)\\\\t\\\\nKrishna Aayush, Gurvendra Pal Singh, Rahul Agarwal, Rahul Saxena\\\\t\\\\nIndia\\\\n\\\\n4. Dr. Ramendra Pati Pandey (Co-PI)\\\\t\\\\nEpigenetic Modulation of ATAD2 Bromodomain: A Precision Therapeutics Approach for Patient-Centric Cancer Treatment in Head & Neck Malignancy\\\\t\\\\nDr. Neeraj Kumar Mishra\\\\t\\\\nGITAM, Vishakhapatnam, India\\\\n\\\\n5. Dr. Parth Sarthi Sen Gupta\\\\t\\\\nMolecular docking and MD simulation of different phytochemicals (Consultancy)\\\\tIndus Biotech (Sponsoring Agency)\\\\t\\\\nIndus Biotech\\\\n\\\\n6. Dr. Sidhartha Singh\\\\t\\\\nMolecular insights into TGF-B signalling pathway disruption by nonsynonymous SNPs variants in COPD: an in silico and MD simulation study\\\\t\\\\nNidhi Mahajan, Vishal Chopra, Kranti Garg, Siddharth Sharma\\\\t\\\\nThapar Institute of Engineering and Technology, Patiala, India\\\\n\\\\n7. Dr. Sidhartha Singh \\\\t\\\\nModeling hexavalent chromium removal from aqueous systems using a fixed bed of immobilized bacteria and zerovalent iron nanoparticles\\\\t\\\\nShashank Garg, Amit Jaiswal, Sanchita Sarkhel, Jastin Samuel, Joginder Singh\\\\t\\\\nLovely Professional University, India\\\\n\\\\n8. Dr. Sidhartha Singh \\\\t\\\\nGenotyping and Molecular Dynamic Simulations Reveal the Role of MSH2 DNA Repair Polymorphisms in Lung Cancer Risk\\\\t\\\\nN. Singh, S. Sharma\\\\t\\\\nThapar Institute of Engineering and Technology, Patiala, India\\\\n\\\\n9. Dr. Surabhi Sonam\\\\t\\\\nEnsemble techniques for predictive modeling of leishmanial activity via molecular fingerprints\\\\t\\\\nSaif Nalband, Pallavi Kiratkar, Maulik Gupta, Mansi Gambhir, Femi Robert, A. Amalin Prince\\\\t\\\\nThapar Institute of Engineering and Technology, Patiala, India\\\\n\\\\n10. Dr. Parth Sarthi Sen Gupta\\\\t\\\\nMechanistic insights into ESBL activity of subclass A2 in Class A betalactamase revealing a distinct strategy towards conferring drug resistance\\\\t\\\\nRiya Karan, Anish Pyne, Saroj K. Panda, Saugata Hazra\\\\t\\\\nIndian Institute of Technology (IIT) Roorkee\\\\n\\\\n11. Dr. Parth Sarthi Sen Gupta\\\\t\\\\nComparative binding efficacy of Ivermectin and Remdesivir against the spike protein of Omicron variants: An in silico perspective\\\\t\\\\nRitwik Patra, Saroj Kumar Panda, Malay Kumar Rana, Suprabhat Mukherjee\\\\t\\\\nKazi Nazrul University; IISER Berhampur\\\\n\\\\n12. Dr. Parth Sarthi Sen Gupta\\\\t\\\\nDual Inhibition of IDO1 and TDO: A Unified Therapeutic Strategy to Combat Alzheimer's Disease and Cancer\\\\t\\\\nSuprabhat Mukherjee\\\\t\\\\nKazi Nazrul University\\\\n\\\\n13. Dr. Parth Sarthi Sen Gupta\\\\t\\\\nDecoding Ceftazidime-Avibactam Resistance in newly identified Klebsiella pneumoniae Carbapenemase (KPC) 107 Variant by Modulating the Active Site Environment\\\\t\\\\nRiya Karan, Saugata Hazra\\\\t\\\\nIndian Institute of Technology (IIT) Roorkee\\\\n\\\\n14. Dr. Meena Pandey\\\\t\\\\nA comprehensive tissue specific analysis of antioxidant potential and metabolite profiling of a vulnerable therapeutic herb Ceropegia sahyadrica\\\\tBhushan B. Dholakia, Purva S. Saraf, Sachin A. Punekar & Ashok P. Giri\\\\t \\\\nCSIR-National Chemical Laboratory, India\\\\n\\\\n15. Dr. Ramendra Pati Pandey\\\\t\\\\nBacteriophages, Antimicrobial Resistance and One Health: An Integrated Approach\\\\t\\\\nUrmi Bajpai, Madhulika Bajpai, Pawan Sharma\\\\t\\\\nDr. B.R. Ambedkar Center for Biomedical Research (ACBR), University of Delhi\\\\n\\\\n16. Dr. Ramendra Pati Pandey\\\\t\\\\nMicrobiota Transplantation as a Future Novel Therapeutic Strategy Approach\\\\t\\\\nSuresh Kumar, Himanshu, Pratibha Gaur, Saheem Ahmad,Paridhi Puri,V. Samuel Raj\\\\t\\\\nNational Institute of Biologicals, Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Noida India\\\\n\\\\n17. Dr. Sidhartha Singh\\\\t\\\\nMolecular modelling and simulation-based profiling of nsSNPs in hedgehog pathway genes linked to COPD susceptibility\\\\t\\\\nDr. Siddharth Sharma\\\\t\\\\nThapar Institute of Engineering and Technology, Patiala, India\\\\n\\\\n18. Dr. Sidhartha Singh\\\\t\\\\nProduction and Optimization of Bacterial Nano Cellulose using Kosakonia radicincitans wava 1 Over Fruit Waste Derived Media\\\\t\\\\nDr. Shashank Garg\\\\t\\\\nLovely Professional University, Phagwara, Punjab, India.\\\\n\\\\n19. Dr. Suvaithenamudhan S\\\\t\\\\nGenomic analysis identifies unreported adaptive mutations in monkeypox virus immune evasion genes\\\\t\\\\nDr. Saravanan KM\\\\t\\\\nB-Aatral Biosciences Private Limited, Bangalore, India\\\\n\\\\n20. Dr. Pravin Savata Gade\\\\t\\\\nXylooligosaccharide in combination with Lactobacillus rhamnosus ameliorates disturbed circadian rhythm and associated neurological internal desynchronization\\\\t\\\\nRutuja Murlidhar Sonkar, Praveena Bhatt\\\\t\\\\nCSIR-Central Food Technological Research Institute, India\\\\n\\\\n21. Dr. Parth Sarthi Sen Gupta\\\\t\\\\nIntegrated experimental and computational evaluation of novel benzopyran-2-one congeners as reactive oxygen species scavengers in inflammation\\\\t\\\\nAnil K. Saxena\\\\t\\\\nCSIR-CDRI, Lucknow, India\\\\n\\\\n22. Dr. Parth Sarthi Sen Gupta\\\\t\\\\nA simple and sensitive colorimetric biosensor based on three different hydrothermally synthesized AuNP-CQD nanocolloids functionalized with aptamer for the biosensing of diclofenac in serum samples\\\\t\\\\nDr. Sanjay Kumar\\\\t\\\\nNational Forensic Sciences University, Gandhinagar, Gujarat, India","functionalMousCount":"4","auditObservations":"The School of Biosciences and Bioengineering has developed a conducive academic environment supported by competent faculty members and laboratory infrastructure catering to teaching and research in biosciences and bioengineering. The curriculum addresses important domains of biotechnology and allied disciplines, while students are provided opportunities for laboratory-based learning and research projects. The School has initiated collaborations with industries and research organizations; however, these interactions require further expansion to enhance experiential learning, translational research, and innovation. Academic processes are generally well established, though additional emphasis on research output, industry engagement, and international collaborations would further enhance the School's academic standing.","auditRecommendations":"• Strengthen OBE implementation through systematic CO–PO mapping, attainment analysis, and evidence-based continuous improvement.\\n• Laboratories may be upgraded by  incorporating advanced instrumentation supporting molecular biology, bioinformatics, genomics, synthetic biology, biomedical engineering, and computational biology.\\n• Expand industry collaborations with biotechnology, pharmaceutical, healthcare, food processing, and biomedical organizations for internships, sponsored projects, and collaborative research.\\n• Introduce emerging interdisciplinary courses in AI for Healthcare, Precision Medicine, Bioinformatics, Synthetic Biology, Regulatory Affairs, and Bioprocess Engineering.\\n• Increase externally funded research projects, patents, technology commercialization, and startup incubation activities.\\n• Promote collaborative research involving engineering, design, healthcare, and life sciences to address societal challenges.\\n• Strengthen biosafety practices, laboratory SOPs, equipment calibration records, and documentation related to research ethics.\\n• Enhance student participation in national and international conferences, innovation competitions, and research publications.\\n• Establish international academic collaborations through faculty exchange, joint research programmes, and collaborative publications.\\n• Develop structured alumni engagement mechanisms for mentoring, internships, and career guidance.","auditDocumentation":[],"__reportArchive":{"category":"internal","auditCycle":"2025-26","version":1},"__auditSignOff":{"submittedBy":{"name":"Dr Ramendra Pati Pandey","designation":"Director","role":"director","date":"2026-07-28T11:19:56.548Z"},"approvedBy":{"name":"","designation":"","role":"","date":""},"auditedBy":{"name":"Dr Abhijeet Digalwar","designation":"External Academic Auditor","role":"academic-external-auditor","email":"akd@pilani.bits-pilani.ac.in","date":"2026-08-13T04:55:50.487Z"}}}	{"studentStrength":[{"Sr No":"1","Class":"B. Tech Bioengineering First year","No. of Student":"106","Total":""},{"Sr No":"2","Class":"B. Tech Bioengineering Second year","No. of Student":"59","Total":""},{"Sr No":"3","Class":"B. Tech Bioengineering Third year","No. of Student":"101","Total":""},{"Sr No":"4","Class":"B. Tech Bioengineering Fourth year","No. of Student":"103","Total":""},{"Sr No":"5","Class":"M.Sc Medical Biotechnology First year"