44	swapnil.bhurat@dypiu.ac.in	academic	SOCE	Dr Swapnil Bhurat	2026-07-10 11:09:09.769789	APPROVED	Dear Sir / Madam,\n\nPlease go through the observations and recommendations provided by the Internal Auditor and prepare the compliance report to be presented before the External Auditor.\n\nYou are requested to include the compliance report for the observations shared in January 2026, along with the observations and recommendations from the latest Internal Audit Report. \nKindly ensure that the compliance report is complete and supported by the necessary documents/evidence, wherever applicable.	IQAC	2026-07-22 06:10:31.002768	{"schoolName":"School of Continuing Education","establishmentYear":"2023","address":"D Y Patil International University, Sector 29, Akurdi, Pune,411044","directorName":"Dr Swapnil Bhurat","directorEmail":"swapnil.bhurat@dypiu.ac.in","ugIntake":"150","pgIntake":"60","academicCalendar":"Available","bosMeetingsCount":"2","stakeholderFeedback":"Based on the employer feedback forms from Gill Plants Pvt. Ltd., Dana Anand India Pvt. Ltd., and Mahle Anand Thermal Systems, the overall feedback is positive. Employers consistently rated the curriculum, graduate preparedness, technical competence, communication, teamwork, and professionalism highly (mostly 4–5 on a 5-point scale). Their suggestions mainly focus on enhancing practical exposure, industry-specific software, certification programmes, and application-oriented learning.","feedbackAnalysis":"The overall feedback received from students, alumni, and employers was highly positive, indicating that the curriculum is relevant, comprehensive, and aligned with current industry requirements.\\nStudents, alumni, and employers appreciated the strong theoretical foundation, industry-oriented curriculum, and outcome-based teaching-learning process, which has contributed to improved employability and professional competence.\\nA significant proportion of respondents expressed satisfaction with the curriculum relevance, assessment methods, practical learning, communication skills, and graduate preparedness for employment and higher studies.\\nStakeholders recommended greater emphasis on practical learning, laboratory sessions, industrial visits, internships, live projects, and industry-sponsored activities to further strengthen experiential learning.\\nFeedback highlighted the need to continuously incorporate emerging technologies such as Artificial Intelligence (AI), Machine Learning (ML), Data Analytics, Industry 4.0, Robotics, Automation, Electric Vehicle technologies, CAD/CAE tools, Power BI, PLC programming, MATLAB/Simulink, and digital engineering tools into the curriculum.\\nEmployers acknowledged that graduates possess good technical knowledge, professional ethics, teamwork, and problem-solving abilities, while recommending more application-oriented learning and industrial exposure.\\nOverall, the feedback indicates that the curriculum effectively meets stakeholder expectations while identifying opportunities for continuous curriculum enhancement through stronger industry collaboration, practical exposure, emerging technology integration, and skill-based learning, thereby further improving graduate employability and programme quality.","actionTakenReport":"The feedback received from students, alumni, and employers has been systematically analyzed and utilized for continuous curriculum improvement. The recommended actions focus on enhancing industry relevance, practical exposure, emerging technologies, employability skills, and stakeholder engagement, thereby supporting the university's commitment to Outcome-Based Education (OBE) and continuous quality improvement.","foreignCollaborations":"1.\\tMoon Mission\\nTeam Members:  Mr. Dinesh Kumar, Dr. Swapnil Bhurat, Dr. Gaurav Kumar Singh\\n\\nPartner\\tRole & Expertise\\tKey Background\\nDr. Lutz Richter (Germany/ESA Expert)\\tPlanetary Robotics & Mobility\\tA veteran of ESA, NASA, and JAXA missions (ExoMars, Rosetta). He is a specialist in terramechanics—how rover wheels interact with lunar/Martian soil.\\nDr. Tze Chuen (T.C.) Ng (Hong Kong)\\tPrecision Sampling Tools\\tKnown for designing \\"space dental drills\\" and sampling tools used in major missions like Beagle 2 and the Chinese Chang’e lunar missions.\\nDr. Prabhat Ranjan (DYPIU, India)\\tSystems Integration & In Situ Research\\tFormer Vice Chancellor of D.Y. Patil International University (DYPIU). He brings a background in nuclear fusion, sensor networks, and \\"Technology Vision\\" for long-term space research.\\nKey Research Areas\\nThe synergy between these three experts focuses on the \\"In Situ Resource Utilization\\" (ISRU) cycle—finding, excavating, and testing materials on the Moon's surface.\\n1. Lunar Excavation & Mineral Extraction\\nThe collaboration works on the mechanics of excavating lunar regolith (soil). Dr. Richter’s expertise in how rovers move on dusty, low-gravity terrain is paired with Dr. T.C. Ng’s specialized tools to create systems that can dig for minerals without getting stuck or damaging the equipment.\\n2. Advanced Space Sampling Tools\\nDr. T.C. Ng’s work is pivotal here. Unlike standard drills, space tools must operate in a vacuum with extreme temperature swings. The research focuses on:\\n•\\tMicro-sampling: Taking tiny, precise cores of lunar rock.\\n•\\tContamination-free retrieval: Ensuring minerals are excavated and stored for testing without being altered by the tool itself.\\n3. In Situ Testing and Analysis\\nRather than just bringing samples back to Earth, the collaboration explores how to test minerals on the Moon. This includes:\\n•\\tIdentifying volatiles (like water ice) in the lunar polar regions.\\n•\\tTesting the structural properties of regolith for potential use in 3D printing lunar bases.\\n4. Space Rover Autonomy\\nUnder Dr Prabhat Ranjan’s guidance at DYPIU, the research incorporates advanced sensor networks and system logic to allow rovers to operate more autonomously. This is crucial for excavating areas where real-time control from Earth is difficult due to signal lag.\\n\\nResearch Paper: (Dr Ram Kunwer)\\nKumar, A., Kunwer, R., Kanojia, N. et al. Thermal and hydrodynamic characteristics of Therminol VP-1 oil flow across perforated conical hollow turbulence promoter in Scheffler dish receiver tube. Sci Rep 15, 45720 (2025). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-28251-8","nationalCollaborations":"","functionalMousCount":"5","__auditSignOff":{"submittedBy":{"name":"Dr Swapnil Bhurat","designation":"Director","role":"director","date":"2026-07-10T11:09:09.687Z"},"approvedBy":{"name":"IQAC","designation":"IQAC","role":"iqac","date":"2026-07-22T06:10:31.359Z"},"auditedBy":{"name":"Dr Anuradha Patil","designation":"Internal Academic Auditor","role":"academic-internal-auditor","date":"2026-07-21T06:42:36.315554669"}},"auditObservations":"1. Academic Governance and Statutory Compliance\\na) School profile, programme details and intake records are available. Student strength data should be presented in a consolidated format with programme-wise totals and a single grand total.\\nb) Board of Studies records are available. However, only the BoS meetings pertaining to AY 2025–26 should be included. The BoS conducted in April should be excluded as it falls outside the assessment period.\\nc) Stakeholder feedback has been collected. However, the corresponding Action Taken Report (ATR) should be documented.\\nd) NEP 2020 initiatives have been implemented. \\n\\n2. Outcome-Based Education (OBE) Implementation\\na) OBE implementation records are available.\\nb) Indirect attainment records for the M.Tech (EV) 2024 batch are not available.\\nc) Complete attainment analysis and evidence of continuous improvement through ATRs should be maintained.\\n\\n3. Best Practices and Quality Initiatives\\na) Best practices have been identified and implemented.\\nb) Documentation of online teaching practices may be further strengthened.\\n\\n4. Student Mentoring and Academic Support Systems\\na) Faculty-wise mentoring allocation records are available.\\nb) Batch-wise graduating student records are available and verified.\\n\\n5. Student Development and Progression\\na) Records of MOOCs, skill development and value-added courses are available.\\nb) Alumni interaction records are available. However, the Valedictory Function report should include the necessary approvals and signatures.\\nc) Viewer access should be enabled for shared digital records wherever applicable.\\nd) Records of activities conducted under clubs/professional bodies along with the list of student members are not available.\\n\\n6. Faculty Performance, Research and Professional Development\\na) Faculty Development Programme records are available.\\nb) For the Five-Day FDP on CFD, DEM, ANSYS and INFINIPOINT, participant feedback is unavailable and viewer access should be enabled for certain certificates.\\nc) Documents should be properly scanned and maintained for effective verification.\\nd) Shared digital links should have appropriate viewer access.\\n\\n7. Research, Consultancy and Collaborations\\na) Reports of activities conducted under signed MoUs are available.\\nb) Activity reports should be maintained for every MoU to demonstrate functional outcomes and institutional impact.\\n","auditRecommendations":"Overall Observations & Recommendation\\na) The School has established the required academic and quality assurance processes.\\nb) Most of the required documents and supporting evidence are available for verification.\\nc) Action Taken Reports (ATRs) should be included for stakeholder feedback.\\ne) Documentation of best practices and activities conducted under signed MoUs should be more comprehensive.\\nf) Research, faculty development, student support activities and NEP 2020 initiatives are progressing satisfactorily.\\nh) Overall, the School demonstrates satisfactory compliance with NAAC quality requirements. Continued efforts towards comprehensive documentation, evidence management and continuous quality improvement will further enhance NAAC preparedness.","auditDocumentation":[{"name":"DYPIU_Academic_Audit_Evaluation_SoCE (1).xlsx","fileName":"DYPIU_Academic_Audit_Evaluation_SoCE (1).xlsx","url":"/uploads/users/24b00ad38ff2ac07/attachments/32a4e281-7694-4ba6-9251-d12081ad263c-DYPIU_Academic_Audit_Evaluation_SoCE__1_.xlsx"},{"name":"Internal Audit Report_SoCE_July 2026.pdf","fileName":"Internal Audit Report_SoCE_July 2026.pdf","url":"/uploads/users/24b00ad38ff2ac07/attachments/3359885f-a56b-4fa0-b52e-e9eca7bad961-Internal_Audit_Report_SoCE_July_2026.pdf"}],"__reportArchive":{"category":"internal","auditCycle":"2025-26","version":1,"approvedOn":"2026-07-22T06:10:31.359Z"}}	{"studentStrength":[{"Sr No":"1","Class":"B. Tech (ME) 2025-28","No. of Student":"42","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"2","Class":"B. Tech (ME) 2024-27","No. of Student":"75","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"3","Class":"B. Tech (ME) 2023-26","No. of Student":"83","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"4","Class":"B. Tech (EE) 2025-28","No. of Student":"40","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"5","Class":"B. Tech (EE) 2024-27","No. of Student":"26","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"6","Class":"B. Tech (EE) 2023-26","No. of Student":"147","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"7","Class":"M. Tech (EV) 2025-27","No. of Student":"40","Total":"486"},{"Sr No":"8","Class":"M. Tech (EV) 2024-26","No. of Student":"33","Total":"486"}],"facultyStrength":[{"Sr No":"1","Required":"24","Available":"12"}],"boardOfStudies":[{"Sr No":"1","Date of the meeting":"30/08/2025 and 13/04/2026","Link for MoM":[{"name":"SoCE BoS Summary.docx.pdf","fileName":"SoCE BoS Summary.docx.pdf","url":"https://storage.googleapis.com/dypiu-schoolappraisal-uploads/users/d291669ed87e0b9f/attachments/d4af52bd-c95d-4f6f-a979-250c685a7c19-SoCE_BoS_Summary.docx.pdf"}]}],"syllabusRevision":[{"Sr No":"1","Category of Feedback":"Student feedback\\t","Link for Analysis and ATR":[{"name":"Curriculum Feedback Form for Students.pdf","fileName":"Curriculum F